            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Egg White Protein Powder Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Egg white protein powder is dried egg white, usually spray-dried from liquid whites separated from the yolk. A large egg white contains about 3.5 to 4 g of protein, so a 25 g serving of protein represents roughly six to seven egg whites. Because the yolk is removed, the powder has essentially no fat and no cholesterol, and very little carbohydrate. The main protein is ovalbumin, alongside ovotransferrin, ovomucoid and lysozyme.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the US, egg products sold as ingredients must be pasteurized under federal rules, and dried egg whites are typically heat-treated after drying as well. That makes egg white powder safe to drink raw in a shake, unlike raw liquid egg whites from the shell, which can carry Salmonella. It also partly denatures the proteins, which is relevant to the avidin question covered below.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not every egg protein is pure white. Some products, such as whole-egg protein powders, include the yolk, which adds fat, cholesterol, vitamins and a richer taste, but also more calories per gram of protein. Others blend egg white with whey or casein, which means they are no longer dairy-free. If you want a dairy-free egg protein, the first ingredient should be egg white or egg albumen, and there should be no milk ingredient anywhere on the list.
          </p>

          <p>
            Egg white is one of the oldest protein supplements on the market, and it was a standard choice for bodybuilders before whey became cheap. Today it is a niche product, mostly bought by people who avoid dairy, react to plant proteins, or follow paleo-style diets that exclude both.
          </p>
        </div>

