                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Leucine per 25 g protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 2.1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 2.5-2.8 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 2 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical price tier</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid to premium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget to mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget to mid</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Taste in water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plain, slightly eggy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mild, milky</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Earthy</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Main allergen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Egg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Milk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Legume / pea</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Leucine figures in the table are rough estimates from typical amino acid profiles and vary by product. The gap is small. Egg white has a little less leucine per gram of protein than whey, so a slightly larger serving, around 30 g of protein rather than 25 g, closes the difference for people who care. For most people eating enough total protein across the day, the source of one shake matters far less than hitting their daily target. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide covers the targets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compared with plant protein, egg white has a more complete profile than any single plant source except soy, and it avoids the heavy-metal concerns that tend to attach to rice and pea proteins grown in contaminated soil. Plant proteins win on price and on suitability for vegans. Our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> comparison and <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> rankings cover those options.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical summary: if you can drink whey, whey is cheaper and slightly better for muscle. If you cannot, egg white is the best animal-based alternative alongside beef protein isolate, which is covered in our <a href="/best-beef-protein-powder/">best beef protein powder</a> guide. If you avoid animal products, choose a plant blend.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="biotin-avidin" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Biotin and Avidin: Is Egg White Protein a Problem?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Raw egg white contains avidin, a protein that binds tightly to biotin, one of the B vitamins, and prevents it from being absorbed. Biotin deficiency from eating raw egg whites is real but rare: the documented cases involve people eating large amounts of raw egg white every day for weeks or months. Symptoms include thinning hair, skin rash and, in more severe cases, neurological effects.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat denatures avidin and greatly reduces its ability to bind biotin, which is why cooked eggs are not a concern. Commercial egg white powder is pasteurized and usually heat-treated after drying, which reduces avidin activity, though the extent varies with the process and brands rarely publish it. That leaves some uncertainty, but a scoop or two a day as part of a normal diet is very unlikely to cause a deficiency, especially if you eat other biotin sources such as whole eggs, nuts, seeds, legumes and meat. Egg yolk is itself one of the richest food sources of biotin.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you plan to use egg white protein heavily, say three or more servings a day for months, it is sensible to make sure your diet includes biotin-rich foods, or to ask a doctor or registered dietitian about whether a supplement makes sense. Cooking with the powder, such as in pancakes or baking, reduces any remaining avidin activity further.
          </p>

          <p>
            One unrelated note people often confuse with this: high-dose biotin supplements can interfere with some laboratory blood tests, including certain thyroid and heart tests. That is a reason to tell your doctor if you take a biotin supplement, not a reason to worry about egg protein. Egg white protein does not supply biotin; it is the yolk that does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="allergy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Egg Allergy and Other Safety Notes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Egg is one of the most common food allergies, particularly in young children, and it is one of the major allergens that must be declared on US food labels. Many children outgrow it, but some do not, and adults can also develop it. If you have a known or suspected egg allergy, do not use egg white protein; talk to an allergist instead. This page is general information, not medical advice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most of the allergenic proteins in egg are in the white, not the yolk. Ovomucoid, one of the main ones, is stable when heated, which is why some people react to baked and dried egg products even after heat treatment. Pasteurized or powdered egg white is not a safe workaround for an allergy. People who tolerate baked egg under an allergist's guidance should still not assume that a concentrated protein powder is equivalent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond allergy, egg white protein is generally well tolerated. It contains no lactose and almost no fermentable carbohydrate, which makes it a good fit for many people with a sensitive stomach. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">best protein powder for sensitive stomach</a> guide covers the additives that cause more trouble than the protein itself. Some flavored egg powders do add gums, lecithin and sweeteners, so read the full list.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two other groups should check with a doctor before adding any protein supplement: people with kidney disease, and people who are pregnant or taking medication. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide covers the evidence on kidneys and contaminants in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="downsides" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Sodium, Foam and Taste: The Practical Downsides
          </h2>

          <p>
            Egg whites are naturally high in sodium, around 50 to 60 mg per large white. Because a 25 g protein serving represents six or seven whites, egg white powder typically carries a few hundred milligrams of sodium per serving even without added salt. NOW lists about 260 mg in its 16 g protein serving and MRM about 370 mg per serving, per their labels and listings. That is not a problem for most active people, but it is worth counting if you are watching salt for blood pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Foaming is the most common complaint. Egg white proteins trap air, which is why whites whip into meringue, and shaking a powder hard in a bottle can produce a thick head of foam. Buyers commonly suggest mixing it gently, stirring rather than shaking, using a blender on low, or letting the shake sit for a minute before drinking. Some flavored products add lecithin or gums partly to manage this.
          </p>

          <p>
            Taste is plain and slightly eggy in unflavored versions, and the texture is thinner than whey. Most people flavor unflavored egg white with cocoa, coffee, fruit or a sweetener of their choice. In smoothies the taste mostly disappears. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-smoothies/">best protein powder for smoothies</a> guide covers that approach, and the <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">best unflavored protein powder</a> guide compares more single-ingredient options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cost is the last downside. Egg white protein generally costs more per gram of protein than whey and most plant blends, and the smaller scoops on some budget tubs can make the per-serving price look better than it is. Compare cost per 25 g of protein, not per tub.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these powders on label analysis, published certification status and typical price tier. We did not run our own lab tests or tasting panels, and nothing on this page should be read as if we did. Where we discuss taste, foaming or mixing, we are summarizing what buyers commonly report. Our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> page explains our method.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first filter was protein source: egg white or egg albumen as the only or main protein, with no whey, casein or other milk protein. We excluded whole-egg and egg-and-whey blends. We then looked at protein per serving relative to calories and serving weight, because scoop sizes vary widely in this category, and at the ingredient list, favoring products with few additives and clearly listed sweeteners.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification was harder to find than in the whey category. We could not confirm an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing for any of these products at the time of writing. We noted brand claims, such as NOW's in-house testing program and MRM's non-GMO and gluten-free certifications, but they are not the same as banned-substance testing. Drug-tested athletes should check the certifier databases directly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally we considered price per 25 g of protein, using budget, mid and premium tiers because prices change often. Every pick has at least two concrete drawbacks, listed on its card, and none is the right choice for everyone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Use Egg White Protein: Shakes, Baking and Dose
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a shake, mix one serving with water or a non-dairy milk, stir or blend gently to limit foam, and add flavor if you bought unflavored. A serving of around 25 to 30 g of protein is a reasonable target after training or as part of a meal. Our guide to <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a> explains why total daily protein matters more than timing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Egg white powder is one of the best protein powders for baking and cooking, because egg already plays a structural role in many recipes. It works in pancakes, waffles, protein muffins, oats and homemade bars, and it helps bind and lighten batters. Judee's is sold mainly for this purpose, and it can also be reconstituted with water as a substitute for fresh whites in some recipes. Cooking also removes any lingering concern about avidin.
          </p>

          <p>
            How much you need depends on your total protein target, not on the powder. Most active adults do well somewhere around 1.2 to 2.0 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day, mostly from food. One or two scoops of egg white protein a day is a convenient way to top up, and there is no evidence-based reason to drink more than that.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, store it like any protein powder: sealed, cool and dry. Egg white powder is shelf-stable and has a long shelf life when kept dry, which is one reason it has been used in baking and catering for decades. Check the best-by date on the tub and the bag seal on budget packaging, which buyers sometimes report is less robust than a tub.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing price per tub</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Scoop sizes in egg protein range from about 20 g to 33 g, and protein per scoop from 16 g to 25 g. A cheaper tub with a small scoop can cost more per gram of protein. Divide the price by total protein in the tub, or compare cost per 25 g, before you decide what is cheap.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaking it like whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Egg white proteins trap air, so shaking hard in a bottle produces a thick layer of foam. Stir it, blend on low, or let it rest for a minute before drinking. Mixing with a little liquid first and topping up afterward also helps, as buyers commonly suggest.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming egg protein is allergy-safe because it is dairy-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Egg is one of the most common food allergens, and the allergenic proteins are concentrated in the white. Pasteurization and drying do not remove them. If you have an egg allergy or suspect one, avoid egg white protein and talk to an allergist about alternatives.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a blend by mistake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some egg protein products are blended with whey or casein, which makes them no longer dairy-free. Others are whole-egg powders with the yolk included. If you want a dairy-free egg white protein, the first ingredient should be egg white or albumen and there should be no milk ingredient on the list.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the sodium</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Egg whites are naturally high in sodium, and a full protein serving can carry 250 to 400 mg even without added salt. That is fine for most active people, but if you are watching salt for blood pressure, count it as part of your day and choose an unsalted, unflavored version.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best egg white protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people, Naked Egg is the best egg white protein powder, because it is a single ingredient and delivers about 25 g of protein in about 110 calories per two-scoop serving. NOW Sports is the budget pick with a smaller scoop, and Jay Robb is the best-known flavored option, sweetened with stevia. Check the label of the exact flavor you buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is egg white protein better than whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not for most people. Whey is cheaper and a little higher in leucine, the amino acid most tied to muscle building. Egg white is a high-quality complete protein and the better choice if you avoid dairy or react to lactose. A slightly larger serving of egg white, around 30 g of protein, closes most of the leucine gap.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does egg white protein powder affect biotin?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Raw egg white contains avidin, which binds biotin. Heat reduces this effect, and commercial egg white powder is pasteurized and usually heat-treated. A scoop or two a day in a normal diet is very unlikely to cause biotin deficiency, which has mostly been documented in people eating large amounts of raw egg white for long periods. Ask a dietitian if you use it heavily.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is egg white protein dairy-free?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Pure egg white protein contains no milk and no lactose, so it is dairy-free by ingredients. Some products blend egg with whey or casein, and some are made in facilities that also process milk. If you have a milk allergy, read the ingredient list and the facility statement, and talk to your allergist if your allergy is severe.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can you drink egg white protein powder raw?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. In the US, egg products sold as ingredients must be pasteurized, and dried egg whites are typically heat-treated as well, so egg white protein powder is safe to mix into a shake without cooking. That is different from raw liquid egg whites from the shell, which can carry Salmonella and should be cooked or bought pasteurized.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is egg white protein good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can fit a weight-loss diet well, because it is almost pure protein, with no fat and very little carbohydrate, at roughly 110 calories per 25 g of protein. Protein helps with fullness and preserving muscle while you eat less. It is not special for fat loss, though; total calories and total protein across the day matter more than the source.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Who should not use egg white protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Anyone with an egg allergy should avoid it. People with kidney disease, those who are pregnant, and anyone taking medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement. People watching sodium for blood pressure should count the few hundred milligrams in each serving.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

